A||N||4||lowfierogt||Help!!!!!! AC problem||||12||
Z||000000||lowfierogt||07-30-2001||10:52 PM||||I need to know if you can patch the big AC line.  You know the part the mounts to the frame in the back of car by connecter on left side of car.  Well the connector came loose from frame and wore a hole in big ac line.   Can it be JB welded or something with out replacing line?<P>thanks for the space.<P>||||reg||12
Z||000001||deceler8||07-31-2001||12:18 PM||||I would guess the JBweld isn't gonna work as it won't hold the pressure.<P>One thing that might work would be a cold water pipe repair sleeve from a hardware store...they clamp around the hole or crack.<P>Another option would be to have the hole/crack brazed. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||07-31-2001||07:38 PM||||You can't clamp a patch on it... unless your patch can handle 200-300 hundred PSI, maybe more. That side operates at pressures of 150-200PSI or more depending on weather and other things. <P>Many epoxies do not hold well on aluminum. <P>Only a qualified aluminim shop can repair the Al parts. Even then I don't think I'd trust it much. Especially if it's the high side that is holed. <P>If the rubber is bad that can be fixed by putting new hose on the Al hardware. Has top be done by a shop with the proper crimping equipment.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 07-31-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||lowfierogt||07-31-2001||10:52 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B>You can't clamp a patch on it... unless your patch can handle 200-300 hundred PSI, maybe more. That side operates at pressures of 150-200PSI or more depending on weather and other things. <P>Many epoxies do not hold well on aluminum. <P>Only a qualified aluminim shop can repair the Al parts. Even then I don't think I'd trust it much. Especially if it's the high side that is holed. <P>If the rubber is bad that can be fixed by putting new hose on the Al hardware. Has top be done by a shop with the proper crimping equipment.<P>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 07-31-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I cut the hose can you put a new rubber hose over it with clamps?.  Its on the low side.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||maryjane||08-01-2001||06:38 PM||||Is the hole in the rubber part of the line or in the metal part? If it is in the rubber part, I suppose you could get it spliced. If it is inthe metal, see the above reply. This is one of those things that needs to be done right tho. Consider the high cost of freon.Best bet is a replacment hose or let a a/c shop fix your old one. ||||reg||1
